Most of the PHP I deal with these days is not written by me. An agent writes it, quickly and in large amounts, and my part of the job is to read it and decide whether it is right.

Reading code is a different activity from writing it. When I review a change, the diff tells me what moved, not whether it works. To know that I have to follow the code: from a call to the method it lands on, from an interface to the class that actually runs, from a method to every place that calls it, from a service id to its definition. Review is navigation, repeated hundreds of times.

That is the loop [PHPlumb](https://github.com/lussoluca/phplumb) is built for. It is a code navigation engine for PHP, written in Rust, with thin extensions for VS Code and Zed, and it does one thing: it takes you to the right place in the code, without waiting, in projects of 40,000 files.

